EXT. HARBOR LOT - FOOD TRUCK "ROSA'S RUEDA" - DAY

Sun on chrome. Gulls. Steam rolling out of the order window.

ROSA (50s, apron, forearms like cables) flips tortillas without looking. A line of customers stretches to the road.

DANNY (14) leans out the window with the receipt pad.

DANNY
Two al pastor, one veggie, extra lime.

ROSA
Say please to the lime.

DANNY
Please, lime.

From the cab, SKYE (20s, they/them, braids, bandana) leans back against the dashboard with a clipboard. Skye taps the pen against their teeth.

SKYE
Rosa, the health inspector's coming at noon. They've already emailed.

ROSA
Let him come.

SKYE
They said "her." They're a woman.

ROSA
Then let her come.

INT. FOOD TRUCK - CONTINUOUS

CLOSE ON the grill. Grease popping. The propane gauge ticking toward red.

CHUY (70s, Rosa's father, hat low) sits on an overturned crate by the back door, peeling an orange with a pocketknife.

CHUY
Mija, the tank is low.

ROSA
It's fine, Papá.

CHUY
It is not fine. It is whistling.

Rosa listens. A faint hiss, thin as thread. She glances at Skye.

ROSA
Skye, check the valve.

SKYE
I'm bookkeeping, not plumbing.

DANNY
I'll do it.

ROSA
You'll do nothing. Stay by the window.

Danny bangs the counter, annoyed. A customer chuckles.

EXT. HARBOR LOT - CONTINUOUS

A white sedan pulls into the lot. INSPECTOR WELLS (40s, windbreaker, tablet) steps out and looks at the truck. At the line. At the sky.

WELLS
Good afternoon! Anyone in charge?

Rosa leans out the order window, wiping her hands.

ROSA
Inspector. We are open for business.

WELLS
You have a permit?

ROSA
I have a daughter who keeps all the paperwork.

SKYE
(from the cab)
I'm not her daughter. I'm the accountant.

WELLS
(not looking up)
I'm sure they're lovely. I'll start with the kitchen.

INT. FOOD TRUCK - CONTINUOUS

Wells climbs in. Eyes the grill. Sniffs.

WELLS
Do you smell gas?

ROSA
Only the fresh tortillas.

WELLS
That's gas, ma'am.

CHUY
Mija. Tell him.

ROSA
Papá, please.

ANGLE ON the hose behind the grill. A hairline crack. The hiss now audible.

SKYE
Rosa. The hose.

DANNY
It's been like that since Friday.

Rosa freezes. Slowly turns on her son.

ROSA
Since Friday?

DANNY
I told Abuelo. He said it was okay.

CHUY
I said it would hold.

ROSA
Hold?

WELLS
I'm going to need everyone out of the truck.

ROSA
Nobody is leaving my truck.

WELLS
Ma'am...

ROSA
Thirty years. My husband built this grill. My father taught me this salsa. You don't come in here with a tablet and tell me to leave.

INSERT - THE TANK GAUGE. Needle on red.

SKYE
(low)
Rosa. Please.

ROSA
Don't "please" me. You all knew.

Silence. The hiss grows. Customers outside have gone quiet.

DANNY
Mamá, I'm sorry.

Rosa's shoulders drop. She looks at him. At Chuy. At the hose.

WELLS
The ignition. The pilot's still lit.

BACK TO SCENE:

Rosa lunges for the shutoff valve. Wells grabs her arm.

WELLS (CONT'D)
Don't touch the burner!

SKYE
Everyone OUT!

Chuy rises. Danny grabs his grandfather's arm. Rosa wrenches free and cranks the valve.

The hiss stops.

Everyone stands still.

Then the pilot light FLICKERS, catches the last of the escaped gas, and the grill WHUMPS into a fireball that blows the order window across the lot.

EXT. HARBOR LOT - CONTINUOUS

Smoke boils up. Customers scatter. Gulls explode into the sky.

Danny drags Chuy clear. Skye hauls Wells over the tailgate. Rosa staggers out last, apron smoking, a charred tortilla still in her hand.

She looks at her truck. At the black smear on the asphalt.

ROSA
(quiet)
Well. That's the inspection.

Skye begins to laugh. Danny too. Wells pats out a spark on his sleeve and finally, helplessly, joins them.
